WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.087
Gravity bends light. The Sun bends the light that passes close to it.

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.528
Far beyond the planets, this bent light comes together, as in a lens.

3
00:00:12.953 --> 00:00:15.816
Some scientists propose to put a telescope there,

4
00:00:15.816 --> 00:00:18.386
and take pictures of planets of other stars.

5
00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:22.292
This is called imaging with the solar gravitational lens.

6
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.010
A 2026 paper asks:

7
00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:29.242
in a simple model of this imaging, what limits the detail of the picture?

8
00:00:29.687 --> 00:00:33.614
The paper does not say whether or when a mission could take such a picture.

9
00:00:35.153 --> 00:00:36.721
In the paper's baseline,

10
00:00:36.721 --> 00:00:40.706
the target has an Earth-like surface, and is 30 parsecs away.

11
00:00:41.153 --> 00:00:44.502
The telescope is 650 times as far from the

12
00:00:44.502 --> 00:00:48.409
Sun as the Earth is. Its opening is 1 metre wide.

13
00:00:49.353 --> 00:00:52.342
The telescope moves across the image of the planet,

14
00:00:52.342 --> 00:00:54.627
one step for each pixel of the picture.

15
00:00:55.053 --> 00:01:04.395
The picture has 128 by 128 pixels. One pixel covers 99.5 kilometres of the planet.

16
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:05.623
At each step,

17
00:01:05.623 --> 00:01:08.647
the telescope gets the light of its own pixel. It

18
00:01:08.647 --> 00:01:12.228
also gets a faint blur of light from all the other pixels.

19
00:01:12.653 --> 00:01:18.137
So the data are a mix. A computation must take the mix apart to recover the picture.

20
00:01:19.184 --> 00:01:21.869
The paper looks at three things that limit the picture.

21
00:01:22.318 --> 00:01:27.720
The first is the corona, the faint glow around the Sun. Its light adds random noise.

22
00:01:28.151 --> 00:01:31.265
The second is clouds. They hide parts of the surface,

23
00:01:31.265 --> 00:01:33.792
and they change from one visit to the next.

24
00:01:34.218 --> 00:01:37.625
The third is the shape of the Sun's gravity. It is not

25
00:01:37.625 --> 00:01:41.979
perfectly round. So the lens spreads each point into a small pattern,

26
00:01:41.979 --> 00:01:43.052
called a caustic.

27
00:01:43.484 --> 00:01:47.413
The paper puts all three into one simple model. In this model,

28
00:01:47.413 --> 00:01:51.277
the parts add up, and the random parts follow the bell curve.

29
00:01:51.718 --> 00:01:54.350
For this model, the paper computes a bound:

30
00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.533
the smallest error that an unbiased method can have.

31
00:01:57.951 --> 00:02:02.000
This bound belongs to the model. It is not a limit for every method.

32
00:02:03.518 --> 00:02:06.831
A picture can be split into patterns of different sizes,

33
00:02:06.831 --> 00:02:08.724
from wide patterns to fine ones.

34
00:02:09.151 --> 00:02:12.542
The paper finds the bound for each size of pattern on its own.

35
00:02:12.984 --> 00:02:17.516
Then it removes one barrier at a time, and sees how much the bound goes down.

36
00:02:17.951 --> 00:02:21.944
For the widest patterns, about 1,600 kilometres and larger,

37
00:02:21.944 --> 00:02:24.989
clouds cause the largest loss of information.

38
00:02:25.418 --> 00:02:28.946
For the finest patterns, 200 kilometres and smaller,

39
00:02:28.946 --> 00:02:32.339
the noise from the corona causes the largest loss.

40
00:02:32.784 --> 00:02:37.608
In between, the order changes with the settings. The paper calls it undetermined.

41
00:02:38.051 --> 00:02:41.742
The shape of the Sun's gravity matters too. In one setting,

42
00:02:41.742 --> 00:02:44.745
the information at one size of pattern vanishes.

43
00:02:45.784 --> 00:02:48.323
Which barrier sets the finest detail? That

44
00:02:48.323 --> 00:02:50.922
depends on how we define the finest detail.

45
00:02:51.351 --> 00:02:52.222
For each size,

46
00:02:52.222 --> 00:02:55.207
compare the signal with the error. Call the size

47
00:02:55.207 --> 00:02:58.503
seen if the signal is at least as large as the error.

48
00:02:58.951 --> 00:03:01.350
With one kind of model cloud,

49
00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:06.644
the signal falls below the error only in a band of middle sizes,

50
00:03:06.644 --> 00:03:08.877
from 910 to 531 kilometres.

51
00:03:09.318 --> 00:03:11.438
Rule one goes from wide to fine,

52
00:03:11.438 --> 00:03:16.142
and stops at the first size that is not seen. It stops before the band.

53
00:03:16.584 --> 00:03:19.494
Rule two takes the finest size that is seen,

54
00:03:19.494 --> 00:03:22.204
even after a gap. It jumps over the band.

55
00:03:22.651 --> 00:03:27.507
So, over 120 settings, rule one makes clouds the limit in 93,

56
00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:33.318
and rule two makes the corona the limit in 80. The rest are undetermined.

57
00:03:33.751 --> 00:03:36.424
The answer also changes with the other rules,

58
00:03:36.424 --> 00:03:38.978
the threshold, and the kind of model cloud.

59
00:03:39.918 --> 00:03:42.531
Now take away the clouds and

60
00:03:42.531 --> 00:03:46.917
the shape of the Sun's gravity. Then the finest

61
00:03:46.917 --> 00:03:51.956
size seen is 637 kilometres after 1 year of observing,

62
00:03:51.956 --> 00:03:56.063
354 after 10 years, and 199 after 100 years.

63
00:03:57.584 --> 00:04:00.214
The model's clouds are simple. Real clouds

64
00:04:00.214 --> 00:04:03.093
may differ. So the paper measures real clouds.

65
00:04:03.518 --> 00:04:05.978
It uses pictures of the Earth from EPIC,

66
00:04:05.978 --> 00:04:10.652
a camera on the DSCOVR spacecraft that sees the whole day side of the Earth.

67
00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:13.765
The paper uses four months of 2024:

68
00:04:13.765 --> 00:04:18.512
January, April, July and October. Each day gives two pictures,

69
00:04:18.512 --> 00:04:22.111
near midnight and near noon, in universal time.

70
00:04:22.551 --> 00:04:24.253
For each spot on the Earth,

71
00:04:24.253 --> 00:04:27.719
it takes a low value from the month's pictures. This is

72
00:04:27.719 --> 00:04:31.249
the surface. The rest of each picture is the cloud part.

73
00:04:31.684 --> 00:04:36.212
How fast the measured clouds change from day to day fits the class of the model.

74
00:04:36.651 --> 00:04:38.611
With these clouds in the model,

75
00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:42.152
the barrier that sets the finest detail is again clouds,

76
00:04:42.152 --> 00:04:45.567
the corona, or undetermined, depending on the choices.

77
00:04:45.984 --> 00:04:48.346
But the real clouds do things that the

78
00:04:48.346 --> 00:04:51.454
model does not have. Their values are lopsided and

79
00:04:51.454 --> 00:04:52.511
have heavy tails,

80
00:04:52.511 --> 00:04:55.992
unlike the bell curve. And they stay alike after two and

81
00:04:55.992 --> 00:04:58.229
three days more than the model says.

82
00:04:58.651 --> 00:05:02.150
So the paper treats the bound as a property of the model only.

83
00:05:03.684 --> 00:05:07.544
Next, the paper sends real pictures through the imaging model.

84
00:05:07.984 --> 00:05:10.981
The surface from the first half of each month is the

85
00:05:10.981 --> 00:05:14.553
truth. The pictures from the second half go through the model,

86
00:05:14.553 --> 00:05:17.088
and four methods try to recover the surface.

87
00:05:17.518 --> 00:05:20.734
For each result, the paper divides the error by the bound.

88
00:05:21.151 --> 00:05:25.594
Below two thirds, the result is below the bound. From two thirds to two,

89
00:05:25.594 --> 00:05:29.235
it reaches the bound. Above two, it is away from the bound.

90
00:05:29.684 --> 00:05:34.274
If the ratio and its range of uncertainty do not fall in one category,

91
00:05:34.274 --> 00:05:36.044
the result is undetermined.

92
00:05:36.484 --> 00:05:38.533
Near midnight, universal time,

93
00:05:38.533 --> 00:05:42.424
the set of sizes used for the comparison was empty. So no

94
00:05:42.424 --> 00:05:47.613
comparison was possible. This does not mean that the surface cannot be seen.

95
00:05:48.051 --> 00:05:51.441
In 2024, 22 results were below the bound.

96
00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:56.031
After seeing this, the paper built the bound separately for each time of day.

97
00:05:56.451 --> 00:06:01.478
With this new bound, none of the 22 stayed below. 16 reached the bound,

98
00:06:01.478 --> 00:06:03.177
and 6 were undetermined.

99
00:06:03.618 --> 00:06:08.126
But this choice came after seeing the results. So the paper tested it again,

100
00:06:08.126 --> 00:06:08.957
on other data.

101
00:06:10.484 --> 00:06:12.954
Before it computed anything for 2023,

102
00:06:12.954 --> 00:06:15.157
the paper fixed the new bound and

103
00:06:15.157 --> 00:06:20.698
the methods. It also wrote down what would count as a finding that holds or breaks.

104
00:06:21.118 --> 00:06:23.536
Then it used four months of 2023.

105
00:06:23.984 --> 00:06:28.571
In 2023, no result of the main configuration was below the bound,

106
00:06:28.571 --> 00:06:30.405
in any of the four months.

107
00:06:30.851 --> 00:06:33.676
The results near midnight could not be compared,

108
00:06:33.676 --> 00:06:36.500
again. Some results near noon were undetermined.

109
00:06:36.918 --> 00:06:39.783
The other findings were checked too. Four held

110
00:06:39.783 --> 00:06:43.022
again. One held only when the four months were taken

111
00:06:43.022 --> 00:06:46.136
together. One could be applied in only two months.

112
00:06:46.136 --> 00:06:49.375
One broke in one month. And one could not be tested.

113
00:06:49.818 --> 00:06:53.106
The paper says that 2023 does not make the 2024

114
00:06:53.106 --> 00:06:56.673
statements stronger. It shows what the same method,

115
00:06:56.673 --> 00:06:59.472
fixed in advance, gives on other months.

116
00:07:00.518 --> 00:07:04.975
The bound belongs to the declared model. It is not a limit for every method.

117
00:07:05.418 --> 00:07:08.140
The paper does not say whether or when a mission

118
00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:10.920
could take a picture of a planet of another star.

119
00:07:11.351 --> 00:07:14.981
It uses one camera, one colour of light, two times of day,

120
00:07:14.981 --> 00:07:17.547
and the stated months. It does not extend

121
00:07:17.547 --> 00:07:20.363
these clouds to other planets or other times.

122
00:07:20.784 --> 00:07:22.702
Some things are not in the model,

123
00:07:22.702 --> 00:07:26.362
for example clouds on a round planet, and other kinds of noise.

124
00:07:26.784 --> 00:07:30.112
To learn more, read the paper. The link is on this page.

125
00:07:30.551 --> 00:07:34.452
This video was made with the help of AI. The voice is synthetic.
